+# @deepseek-ai/dsh-usage-query
+
+English | [中文](README.zh.md)
+
+Cross-session token-usage aggregation service (`ctx.usageQuery`): folds provider-reported usage from the logical session corpus and groups it by any combination of day / model / session dimensions. Read-only — the service never creates or resumes an agent, never assembles a prompt, and never sends a provider request.
+
+## Data source
+
+Each `assistant/message` event's final `usage` (or a streaming `assistant/chunk` usage sample) is folded once per `(turn, step)` — the same last-wins rule token-meter's `tokenUsage` projection applies, so a final message usage supersedes the same step's earlier chunk sample instead of double-counting it. A `request/header` event attributes every following sample to its call configuration's `provider/model`; samples without a header carry the `unknown` model label.
+
+Days are the machine's **local calendar days** of the usage events themselves, so a session that spans a midnight boundary contributes to both days. Buckets are disjoint: uncached input, cache read, cache write, and output; reasoning output is reported separately inside the output bucket.
+
+Reads are bounded: session logs are scanned concurrently (bounded at four), sessions created after the requested window are skipped outright, persisted folds are cached against the persistence backend's lightweight log revisions, and persisted sessions are line-scanned from their raw artifacts instead of being replayed event-by-event — a full replay validates and clones every chunk and costs seconds on large sessions, while the three usage-relevant record types (`request/header`, `assistant/message`, usage `assistant/chunk`) are never packed and scan in milliseconds. Live sessions are served from their in-memory snapshot instead: their artifact is appended continuously, and a revision-stable raw read would spin while a writer keeps changing the file.
+
+## Query contract
+
+`ctx.usageQuery.query(request)` takes an inclusive day window (`from`/`to` as local `YYYY-MM-DD`, either absent = unbounded), an optional epoch-ms `asOf` cutoff (only samples at or before that instant count — the web panel captures it when it opens, so usage produced after the open moment stays out of the view until the panel reopens), and a non-empty, duplicate-free `groupBy` subset of `day` / `model` / `session`. The result is one row per distinct group (each row carrying exactly the requested dimensions plus the summed buckets and a `requests` sample count), sorted deterministically by the grouped dimensions, plus the grand total. Malformed requests fail loudly with a descriptive error before the corpus is touched; a composition without `ctx.sessionQuery` fails with a message naming the missing service.
+
+## Configuration
+
+No settings. Any configured key is rejected — the service reads session logs only.
+
+## Composition
+
+```yaml
+- name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-usage-query'
+```
+
+Requires the `sessionQuery` service (a `dsh-session-query` backend such as `dsh-session-query-sqlite`) and, through it, a persistence backend for historical sessions. The web bundle mounts all three.
+
+## Model Experience
+
+None, as the service folds already-logged session events into a client-facing read model and registers no prompt, message, schema, tool, or model call.
+
+#### KV Cache effect
+
+None; the service neither assembles nor sends provider requests.
+
+## Known Limitations and Deferred Work
+
+- **Raw scans trust the storage format** — the line scanner skips anything it does not recognize (packed rows, deltas, torn tails), so a future format that packs usage records would silently undercount until the scanner learns it; the full `readSession` replay remains the fallback for live sessions and non-raw backends.
+- **Counts are log-scoped** — usage of steps whose messages were later compacted away still counts, matching the whole-log semantics of `tokenUsage`.
+- **Model attribution is last-header-wins** — a usage sample is attributed to the newest `request/header` before it; a session that switches models mid-step attributes the sample to the later header.
